.TH AADRAWTEST 1
.SH NAME
aadrawtest \- check and time drawing through an 8-bit coverage mask
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aadrawtest
[
.I font ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Aadrawtest
paints a solid colour through an 8-bit coverage mask, which is what text
through a scalable font is, and checks both what came out and what it
cost.
.PP
.IR Libmemdraw 's
optimized text blit,
.BR chardraw ,
stores the source colour wherever a mask
.I bit
is set.
It used to refuse any mask deeper than one bit, so an antialiased glyph
went through the general per-pixel path instead;
it now takes a coverage mask too, blending rather than storing.
.SS Correctness
For each of
.BR RGB24 ,
.B XRGB32
and
.BR GREY8 ,
a colour is painted over a known destination through a mask whose
coverage runs 0 to 255 across the image, so every value is exercised,
and every byte of the result is compared against
.if n \{\
MUL(a, src) + MUL(255-a, dst)
.\}
computed in Limbo rather than against the C's own idea of it.
A byte the pixel format does not use must come back exactly as it was.
.PP
The arithmetic is one thing and the addressing another: a glyph's mask
rect starts at an offset inside a subfont strip, and
.B byteaddr(mask, mr.min)
with a per-pixel stride is where an origin or stride error would live.
So real text is drawn twice, into
.B RGB24
which takes the fast path and
.B RGBA32
which by design does not, and every red byte is compared.
The general path is the reference and the glyphs are real.
Shifting the coverage path's mask origin by one byte makes both this and
the ramp check fail, which is the control.
.PP
.BR CMAP8 ,
.B RGB16
and
.B RGBA32
are refused by the fast path by design \(em a palette index cannot be
blended, a five-bit channel is not a byte, and a destination alpha
channel needs the general path's arithmetic \(em and go the old way.
.SS Cost
A 512x512 masked blit is timed for
.BR RGB24 ,
.B XRGB32
and
.BR RGBA32 .
Draw operations queue to
.BR /dev/draw ,
so each blit is flushed inside the timed region; without that the
measurement is of how fast requests can be enqueued.
.PP
The microsecond counts alone cannot say whether the fast path is wired
in, because they depend on the machine.
What is asserted is the
.I ratio
between
.BR RGB24 ,
which takes it, and
.BR RGBA32 ,
which by design does not: the first must be at least a third quicker.
With the dispatch disabled that check fails, which is the control.
.PP
Any
.I font
arguments are opened and a string timed through each.
If a TrueType file is present in
.BR /fonts/ttf ,
.I aadrawtest
starts
.IR ttffs (4)
itself and times the first face it serves at 16 and 32 pixels.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
	aadrawtest /fonts/lucidasans/unicode.8.font
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/aadrawtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (2),
.IR tkofftest (1),
.IR ttffs (4),
.B /doc/ui-modernization-plan.md
.SH BUGS
A bitmap font's mask is one bit deep, so it goes down
.BR chardraw 's
older path and never reaches the coverage one.
The glyph comparison therefore says nothing about the new code unless a
.B .ttf
is present in
.BR /fonts/ttf ,
which this tree does not ship: with none, that line passes without having
tested anything, and only the ramp check and the timings have run.
.I Aadrawtest
says so when it happens, but a green run still does not mean the glyph
path was exercised.
.PP
Nothing checks that the formats named above are still refused: were the
fast path to start accepting
.B CMAP8
it would corrupt a palette index and this test would not notice, because
it never draws to one.
.PP
The ramp check uses one source colour and one destination colour.
.PP
The timing of a string is reported and never asserted on, so a font that
became slower would show only to a reader.
